WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.915
A TLS handshake is the setup conversation that lets two endpoints agree on how to protect

00:00:04.915 --> 00:00:05.940
the session.

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:09.025
At a high level, the client and server negotiate protocol options,

00:00:09.025 --> 00:00:12.140
authenticate the server, establish shared session keys,

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:14.580
and then use those keys to protect application data.

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:19.915
The details vary by TLS version and configuration, but the practical roles are stable:

00:00:19.915 --> 00:00:23.420
authentication answers who the peer is, key establishment creates shared key material,

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:25.780
and symmetric protection carries the bulk traffic.

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:29.220
Server authentication usually depends on certificates and certificate chains.

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:31.990
A certificate binds a public key to a name or identity,

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:35.640
and the client evaluates a chain of trust back to a trusted authority.

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:38.995
That chain may involve certificate authorities, intermediate certificates,

00:00:38.995 --> 00:00:43.160
operating system trust stores, browser policy, enterprise inspection devices,

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:45.865
and application-specific trust decisions.

00:00:45.865 --> 00:00:49.840
Changing signature algorithms or certificate formats can therefore affect more than one

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:50.600
server.

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:51.880
Key establishment solves a different problem.

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:55.090
It helps the endpoints derive session keys that protect the connection.

00:00:55.090 --> 00:00:57.625
Digital signatures help prove identity and integrity;

00:00:57.625 --> 00:01:00.620
they do not by themselves encrypt all application traffic.

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:04.640
Symmetric encryption and integrity protection then protect the data after the handshake.

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:07.385
Forward secrecy concepts add another important property:

00:01:07.385 --> 00:01:11.450
compromise of a long-term key should not automatically expose older captured sessions when

00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:13.665
the protocol is configured correctly.

00:01:13.665 --> 00:01:16.110
Client authentication is another TLS use case,

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:20.615
often seen in mutual TLS for service-to-service traffic, administrative interfaces,

00:01:20.615 --> 00:01:22.725
device identity, or partner integrations.

00:01:22.725 --> 00:01:27.090
Because TLS roles are layered together, changing cryptography can affect clients, servers,

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:30.830
proxies, libraries, certificate issuance, compliance evidence, monitoring,

00:01:30.830 --> 00:01:32.205
and troubleshooting.

00:01:32.205 --> 00:01:35.820
Migration planning starts by asking what role the cryptography plays in each connection,

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:38.310
not by assuming every TLS use case is the same.
